# Symbulator circuit book

title: Circuits for Lesson 12 of the Tutorial

[AS7's Example 16.1 (in TR, the one-step way)]

e,1,0,t2s(u(t))
r1,1,2,1
r2,2,o,5
c,2,0,1/3,0
l,o,0,1,0

analysis: tr
note: This problem does not need the s-domain at all: the source is a function of time and so is the wanted answer. The t2s around the value is because version 9 reads TR sources in the s-domain.
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[AS7's Example 16.1 (in FD, everything as an impedance)]

e,1,0,1/s
r1,1,2,1
r2,2,o,5
r3,2,0,3/s
r4,o,0,s

analysis: fd
note: The capacitor written as 3/s and the inductor as s, to show it can be done. Only valid because the initial conditions are zero.
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[AS7's Example 16.1 (in FD, farads and henries)]

e,1,0,1/s
r1,1,2,1
r2,2,o,5
c,2,0,1/3,0
l,o,0,1,0

analysis: fd
note: The same answer with the schematic's own units, which is what you would actually type.
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[AS7's Example 16.1 (in FD, and back to time)]

e,1,0,t2s(u(t))
r1,1,2,1
r2,2,o,5
c,2,0,1/3,0
l,o,0,1,0

analysis: fd
note: t2s on the way in, s2t on the way out. Both work anywhere an expression is accepted.
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: s2t(vo)


[AS7's Practice Problem 16.1]

e,1,0,t2s(u(t))
r1,1,2,6
c,1,0,1/4,0
l,1,o,2,0
r2,o,0,3

analysis: fd
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: s2t(vo)


[NR11's Example 13.7 (transfer function)]

e,1,0,vs
r1,1,2,1'k
c,2,0,1'µ
r2,2,o,1'k

analysis: fd
note: A transfer function needs no special tool: solve with a symbolic source and divide.
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: vo/vs
